{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Let's first find the expression for $g'(x)$ and then evaluate it at $x=1$.\n\nThe derivative of $g$ can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is a negative number.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}g'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{-10}\\right)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-10x^{-10-1}\\qquad\\gray{\\text{The power rule}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-10x^{-11}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"So we found that $g'(x)=-10x^{-11}$, which can also be written as $-\\dfrac{10}{x^{11}}$.\n\nNow let's plug $\\blueD{x=1}$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n-\\dfrac{10}{(\\blueD{1})^{11}}\u0026=-\\dfrac{10}{1}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-10\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $g'(1)=-10$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $g(x)=x^{-10}$.\n\n$g'(1)=$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":null,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":-10}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"g prime of 1","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
